WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.840
Every day, millions of people prove they
are human by reading a word no one can

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:06.822
quite explain.

00:00:06.822 --> 00:00:12.105
The picture is twisted, the letters bleed
into each other, and still, you read it in

00:00:12.105 --> 00:00:13.410
about three seconds.

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:20.504
This is the story of how a machine learns
to do the same thing, and why the hardest

00:00:20.504 --> 00:00:23.207
part is not seeing the letters.

00:00:23.207 --> 00:00:23.207
It is trusting what you see.

00:00:23.207 --> 00:00:30.116
Behind the scenes, a real team is racing
to build exactly this: a reader for

00:00:30.116 --> 00:00:33.616
thousands of hard, distorted captchas.

00:00:33.616 --> 00:00:34.929
Their scoreboard is honest to the decimal.

00:00:34.929 --> 00:00:40.129
On the easy suite, the reader gets
seventy-five out of a hundred words

00:00:40.129 --> 00:00:41.227
exactly right.

00:00:41.227 --> 00:00:44.629
On the hard suite, twenty-five.

00:00:44.629 --> 00:00:47.338
The target written on the wall is
ninety-eight, and ninety-five.

00:00:47.338 --> 00:00:49.834
That gap is our story.

00:00:49.834 --> 00:00:52.186
Here is a captcha from their test set.

00:00:52.186 --> 00:00:56.381
Six characters, in families the team named
themselves.

00:00:56.381 --> 00:00:59.730
The snow family, letters buried under
speckle.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.388
The split family, with seams that slice
through a glyph.

00:01:04.388 --> 00:01:10.335
And the strippy family, striped shadows
dragging across the text.

00:01:10.335 --> 00:01:11.704
The machine sees pixels.

00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:12.938
Step one: find the ink.

00:01:12.938 --> 00:01:18.466
The image turns gray, contrast is
stretched, and a threshold decides which

00:01:18.466 --> 00:01:20.530
pixels count as foreground.

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:26.986
This is binarization, and every later
decision is chained to it.

00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:32.330
Get it wrong, and the machine is reading a
different picture than you are.

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:39.132
On the dark-on-dark wall, the team's own
reader shattered the word VIOLET into

00:01:39.132 --> 00:01:40.079
fragments.

00:01:40.079 --> 00:01:42.170
Step two: cut the ink into pieces, ideally
one letter each.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:49.694
The simplest approach is connected
components: every blob of ink is a

00:01:49.694 --> 00:01:50.876
candidate.

00:01:50.876 --> 00:01:50.876
But distortion breaks that rule.

00:01:50.876 --> 00:01:57.319
A capital M can over-split into three
separate stems, and the team recorded

00:01:57.319 --> 00:02:00.710
exactly that: an M arriving as L, V, L.

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:05.670
Seventy-seven hard words fail; sixty-three
right here.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:07.659
Step three: name each piece.

00:02:07.659 --> 00:02:13.343
A classic method keeps a library of
rendered prototypes, and slides every

00:02:13.343 --> 00:02:17.567
candidate over each template, scoring
pixel agreement.

00:02:17.567 --> 00:02:19.499
The highest score wins.

00:02:19.499 --> 00:02:24.740
It scored a lowercase L at zero point nine
nine four six.

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:30.399
Then come the rules layered on top: a
lexicon that prefers real words, case

00:02:30.399 --> 00:02:35.387
heuristics, and a small neural network
voting on ambiguous pieces.

00:02:35.387 --> 00:02:40.616
Sensible additions, except the evidence
pack the team published shows these rules

00:02:40.616 --> 00:02:42.146
breaking correct reads.

00:02:42.146 --> 00:02:47.718
A near-perfect lowercase L, at zero point
nine nine four six, gets raised to a

00:02:47.718 --> 00:02:50.751
capital L because the word looks all-caps.

00:02:50.751 --> 00:02:56.438
A correct digit seven loses to a letter G
on a margin of zero point zero four four.

00:02:56.438 --> 00:02:59.914
A correct G gets overruled by the neural
vote.

00:02:59.914 --> 00:03:03.170
Three rules, three own goals.

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:07.090
This is not a bug in one system; it is the
nature of the problem.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:14.514
A nineteen ninety-six survey by Casey and
Lecolinet put it plainly: segmentation

00:03:14.514 --> 00:03:19.128
errors, not shape distortion, dominate
reading failure.

00:03:19.128 --> 00:03:21.086
The team's census agrees.

00:03:21.086 --> 00:03:26.388
The word carBon came back as C, A, F, M: a
split seam cut the B's spine.

00:03:26.388 --> 00:03:31.714
The scores do not drift randomly; they
come in walls.

00:03:31.714 --> 00:03:33.332
Easy, seventy-five exact.

00:03:33.332 --> 00:03:34.138
Hard, twenty-five.

00:03:34.138 --> 00:03:37.879
Dark-on-dark fails at the mask.

00:03:37.879 --> 00:03:40.352
Strippy shreds glyphs into slivers.

00:03:40.352 --> 00:03:45.479
Here is what the team insists on: every
failure is itemized.

00:03:45.479 --> 00:03:50.746
When a clever rule turns out to be wishful
thinking, it gets deleted.

00:03:50.746 --> 00:03:54.369
Nothing is fudged to reach the wall.

00:03:54.369 --> 00:04:00.656
Progress looks like seventy-three, then
seventy-four, then seventy-five: one

00:04:00.656 --> 00:04:02.534
honest item at a time.

00:04:02.534 --> 00:04:07.461
And speed matters: a captcha rarely waits
fifteen seconds.

00:04:07.461 --> 00:04:12.755
End to end, the team's demo medians five
hundred ninety-one milliseconds.

00:04:12.755 --> 00:04:15.930
Every probe stayed inside the ten-second
bar.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:21.565
Even the slowest family: one point three
six seconds.

00:04:21.565 --> 00:04:24.104
So what does building a reader teach?

00:04:24.104 --> 00:04:27.262
First: the picture is the argument.

00:04:27.262 --> 00:04:31.049
Every claim is bound to an image you can
regenerate from a seed.

00:04:31.049 --> 00:04:34.043
Second: measure the wall, not your hope.

00:04:34.043 --> 00:04:39.337
Families and floors replaced every vague
it-looks-better.

00:04:39.337 --> 00:04:44.959
And third: the rules you add to fix one
error can quietly break the ones you

00:04:44.959 --> 00:04:45.908
already had.

00:04:45.908 --> 00:04:49.771
Zero point nine nine four six. Overruled.

00:04:49.771 --> 00:04:51.682
The wall still stands.

00:04:51.682 --> 00:04:57.023
But every failure has a name, a family,
and a next experiment.

00:04:57.023 --> 00:05:02.680
When the next improvement lands, it will
have to reproduce from a seed, survive

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:07.065
three runs, and beat the whole suite
without going backwards.

00:05:07.065 --> 00:05:14.003
That is how you read the unreadable: one
honest, reproducible item at a time.

00:05:14.003 --> 00:05:19.298
All data and scores come from the team's
released, rerunnable evidence.

00:05:19.298 --> 00:05:20.280
Thanks for watching.
